WEBVTT

05:03.710 --> 05:07.412
Good morning, everyone, and welcome to our special council meeting on March the

05:07.912 --> 05:11.330
11th, 2026. The purpose

05:11.830 --> 05:15.290
of this meeting is to discuss water related matters. I will now call

05:15.790 --> 05:18.810
on Tim Brubacher, Regional Clerk, to conduct the roll call. Thank you,

05:19.310 --> 05:22.930
Chair Redman. Present. Councillor Craig. Councillor Erb.

05:23.370 --> 05:26.970
Councillor Foxton. Present. Councillor Gowing. Good morning.

05:27.130 --> 05:30.170
Councillor Harris. Present. Councillor Huinink. Present.

05:30.650 --> 05:34.010
Councillor James. Present. Councillor Liggett.

05:36.260 --> 05:38.660
I can see Councillor Liggett online. Councillor McCabe.

05:39.220 --> 05:42.660
Councillor Nowak. Councillor Rodrigues.

05:43.540 --> 05:46.820
Councillor Salonen. Councillor Shantz.

05:46.900 --> 05:50.100
Councillor Vrbanovic. Councillor Wolf.

05:50.740 --> 05:54.020
Thank you, chair. I'd like to acknowledge that the

05:54.520 --> 05:58.580
land on which we gather today is the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabe

05:59.080 --> 06:02.300
and the Chonnonton peoples. In doing this, I want to reflect on what

06:02.800 --> 06:06.220
it means to be doing important community work like we're engaging in collectively today,

06:06.380 --> 06:09.260
and also my own individual relationship to the land.

06:10.140 --> 06:14.100
As a settler, one of the most important aspects of my individual journey towards truth

06:14.600 --> 06:18.260
and reconciliation has been to listen. I am continually grateful to

06:18.760 --> 06:21.420
a community that shares their stories and their concerns with me.

06:22.140 --> 06:25.860
As well as chair of the region, I know I carry a large responsibility to

06:26.360 --> 06:29.580
ensure that it is not just myself on an individual level listening

06:30.080 --> 06:33.840
and acting, but that as a government we are listening in creating action with the

06:34.340 --> 06:37.800
community. I'm offering this acknowledgement as one of the many actions I

06:38.300 --> 06:42.360
personally and the region largely is taking on our journey towards reconciliation.

06:42.920 --> 06:46.360
We acknowledge the enduring presence of the Indigenous people with whom

06:46.860 --> 06:50.280
we share this land today, their achievements and their contributions to our

06:50.780 --> 06:54.120
community. Are there any declarations of conflict of interest?

06:56.440 --> 06:59.240
Seeing none, we now will move to delegations.

07:00.200 --> 07:03.640
They are listed on the agenda and I'd like to remind all delegations of

07:04.140 --> 07:07.640
some guidelines for participation. If you're attending in person,

07:07.800 --> 07:11.600
please proceed to the podium when you're called upon. You will have five

07:12.100 --> 07:15.879
minutes to speak if you are registered before the deadline and three minutes if you

07:16.379 --> 07:20.320
registered afterwards. When you see a yellow light, it will indicate

07:20.820 --> 07:24.040
you have one minute remaining and when you see a red light, that means your

07:24.540 --> 07:28.520
time is up. Additionally, some general reminders. If you conduct yourself

07:29.020 --> 07:32.740
in any of the following manners, it will result in an immediate immediate removal from

07:33.240 --> 07:37.140
the meeting, interrupting councillors, staff or other delegations,

07:37.780 --> 07:41.260
derogatory statements about councillors, staff or

07:41.760 --> 07:44.820
others, turning on your camera at inappropriate times,

07:45.380 --> 07:47.780
and hate speech or defamatory comments.

07:48.740 --> 07:52.180
One last reminder for delegations, please do not take offence

07:52.680 --> 07:56.700
if following your presentation there are no questions from council. This is council's

07:57.200 --> 08:00.940
time to really listen and reflect on the opinions and ideas shared with

08:01.440 --> 08:05.380
us by our community members. A quick reminder to members of

08:05.880 --> 08:09.540
the audience, you are asked to maintain order. This means you

08:10.040 --> 08:12.460
may not address council without the permission of the chair,

08:13.020 --> 08:16.420
interrupt anyone, disrupt the meeting with unreasonable noise

08:16.920 --> 08:20.220
or vocal expressions such as applause, booing or cheering,

08:20.700 --> 08:22.940
and displaying signs or posters.

08:24.140 --> 08:27.300
Our first delegation is Larry Masseo of Waterloo

08:27.800 --> 08:31.410
Regional Home Builders Association. I believe you're virtual,

08:31.910 --> 08:35.930
Larry, and you have five minutes. Yes, thank you, Chair Redman,

08:36.430 --> 08:40.050
members of Regional Council. Thanks for the opportunity to speak today.

08:40.850 --> 08:44.290
WRHBA is here this morning to voice

08:44.790 --> 08:48.090
our strong support for Councillor Craig's motion calling

08:48.590 --> 08:52.090
for a fulsome independent investigation into the origins of

08:52.590 --> 08:55.970
the region's current water supply and capacity constraint.

08:56.530 --> 08:59.890
Along with the recommendations to strengthen oversight moving forward.

09:01.010 --> 09:05.490
Water is among the region's most essential public responsibilities.

09:06.130 --> 09:10.050
Residents, businesses, and municipal partners all

09:10.550 --> 09:14.290
rely on transparent forward-looking planning to ensure that growth

09:14.450 --> 09:17.570
is supported by infrastructure that is resilient, safe,

09:17.810 --> 09:21.370
and responsibly managed. Like with many

09:21.870 --> 09:25.360
situations in life, we can't know where we're going, unless we know where

09:25.860 --> 09:29.560
we've been. It is clear that the current constraint has roots,

09:29.800 --> 09:33.360
at least partially, in the understanding of the integrated

09:33.860 --> 09:37.960
water supply system and the methodology implemented for monitoring that

09:38.460 --> 09:42.320
system. The emergency of a water capacity constraint of this

09:42.820 --> 09:46.440
scale, with virtually no prior indication of an emerging issue,

09:47.080 --> 09:50.280
has raised understandable concerns across the community.

09:51.510 --> 09:55.670
Residents and businesses deserve clarity on how this situation

09:55.750 --> 09:58.550
developed, whether warning signs were missed,

09:58.710 --> 10:02.790
and what systemic improvements are needed to prevent similar issues

10:03.290 --> 10:06.590
in the future. An independent, arm's length review is

10:07.090 --> 10:10.390
the most effective way to restore public confidence. Such an

10:10.890 --> 10:14.630
investigation can objectively assess the decision-making processes,

10:15.110 --> 10:18.790
data assumptions, governance structures, and resourcing

10:20.390 --> 10:22.470
contributed to the present constraint.

10:23.190 --> 10:27.430
Importantly, it can also highlight opportunities

10:27.930 --> 10:30.950
to modernize oversight, strengthen internal accountability,

10:31.430 --> 10:34.550
and improve communication among regional

10:35.050 --> 10:39.150
departments, local municipalities, and the public. This is

10:39.650 --> 10:42.950
not about assigning blame. It is about ensuring that our systems are

10:43.450 --> 10:46.880
robust transparent, and capable of supporting the

10:47.380 --> 10:51.280
region's long-term security. The motion before you today reflects

10:51.780 --> 10:55.120
a commitment to responsible stewardship and good

10:55.620 --> 10:59.480
governance. At the same time, the consequences of

10:59.980 --> 11:03.800
development freeze are not abstract. WRHBA,

11:04.300 --> 11:07.480
together with Build Urban, the Grand Valley Construction Association,

11:07.800 --> 11:11.810
and other large community organisations, is in process of

11:12.310 --> 11:16.410
retaining an independent economic consultant to quantify the

11:16.910 --> 11:20.890
social and economic costs associated with a prolonged inability

11:20.970 --> 11:24.650
to bring new housing and employment related development forward.

11:25.370 --> 11:29.130
This work is being conducted entirely at industry cost,

11:29.530 --> 11:33.930
but for the sole purpose of ensuring that council fully understands

11:34.090 --> 11:37.810
the broad community wide impacts of inaction. And why

11:38.310 --> 11:41.650
a timely, transparent resolution to the water

11:42.150 --> 11:45.770
constraint is essential. Embracing an external

11:46.270 --> 11:49.730
review will help ensure that future growth, whether it

11:50.230 --> 11:54.010
be housing, economic development, or public services, proceeds with

11:54.090 --> 11:58.250
confidence in the region's infrastructure planning. So for those reasons,

11:58.570 --> 12:02.330
we urge council to support agenda item 6.1,

12:02.900 --> 12:06.660
and move forward with a comprehensive independent investigation

12:06.980 --> 12:10.780
and subsequent recommendations for a stronger oversight

12:11.280 --> 12:14.740
of the water. Thank you for your time and

12:14.980 --> 12:18.260
if there are any questions, I'll try to answer them for you. Thank you.

12:19.700 --> 12:23.380
Thank you very much, Larry. I see no questions, but I really

12:23.880 --> 12:27.460
do appreciate your coming to express your support.

12:28.670 --> 12:32.470
Thank you. The next delegation is Matthew Parker from

12:32.970 --> 12:33.230
Kitchener.

12:36.350 --> 12:39.070
Matthew, you are in person, I believe.

12:40.190 --> 12:42.990
You have the floor and you have five minutes. Welcome. I see.

12:54.150 --> 12:57.310
Thank you. Chair Redman, members of regional council,

12:57.810 --> 13:00.990
thank you for the opportunity to speak to you today. I'm here as a resident

13:01.490 --> 13:05.230
of Waterloo region, and I'm here because I believe Councillor Gowing's water protection motion

13:05.730 --> 13:08.550
is not just good policy, but it is an urgent necessity.

13:08.950 --> 13:12.630
Let me start with something simple. Every morning, like hundreds of thousands

13:13.130 --> 13:15.950
of people across the region, I turn on my tap and I trust that clean

13:16.450 --> 13:20.040
water will flow. I trust that it will be safe and accessible. That trust,

13:20.540 --> 13:23.680
the kind we barely think of, is the foundation on which our entire community

13:24.180 --> 13:27.720
is built. Our homes, our businesses, our schools, our hospitals,

13:28.220 --> 13:32.120
our future, all depend on water. But that trust is

13:32.620 --> 13:36.080
now in jeopardy as uncertainty has begun to permeate the public discussion

13:36.580 --> 13:40.840
around the issue of water capacity. In December 2025,

13:41.340 --> 13:45.040
regional staff confirmed what many had feared: the Mannheim service area, which supplies

13:45.560 --> 13:49.720
to all of Kitchener, all of Waterloo and parts of Cambridge, Woolwich and Wilmot

13:50.760 --> 13:53.240
has reached a critical water capacity constraint.

13:54.040 --> 13:57.560
Demand is outpacing available supply. The system was

13:58.060 --> 14:01.720
not designed to sustain the pressure it is now under. Let me

14:02.220 --> 14:05.920
put some numbers to this. The total available long-term sustainable water supply

14:06.420 --> 14:09.880
in the Mannheim service area is approximately 1,455

14:10.380 --> 14:14.450
litres per second when all systems are fully operational. With the recommended

14:14.950 --> 14:18.250
20% operational resiliency buffer, the bare minimum needed to allow

14:18.750 --> 14:22.890
for repairs, maintenance and emergencies, that number drops to 1,164

14:23.390 --> 14:27.090
litres per second. Current demand based on building

14:27.590 --> 14:32.010
permits issued through 2024 is already approximately 1,356

14:32.510 --> 14:36.490
litres per second. That means we are consuming more water than we can sustainably produce.

14:37.130 --> 14:41.210
Regional staff have confirmed that we are operating at a negative 6.7%

14:41.710 --> 14:45.080
operational resiliency. I want you to sit with that number for a moment,

14:45.800 --> 14:49.040
-6.7%. We are not approaching a precipice,

14:49.540 --> 14:52.920
we are already past it. Regional Hydrogeology

14:53.000 --> 14:56.640
Manager, Jeff Morrisey, told this very council that water levels are reaching a

14:57.140 --> 15:00.840
point where we could start losing supply wells. Storage tank levels are

15:01.340 --> 15:04.840
dropping more frequently below key thresholds. Groundwater aquifer levels

15:05.340 --> 15:08.680
are already under stress. This is not a theoretical problem,

15:08.920 --> 15:12.570
this is real. This is now. Now some will ask,

15:12.650 --> 15:16.810
what do provincial water taking permits have to do with regional infrastructure constraint?

15:16.890 --> 15:20.370
The answer is everything. Waterloo Region depends on groundwater

15:20.870 --> 15:24.330
for approximately 80% of its drinking water supply. We operate one of

15:24.830 --> 15:27.930
the most complex municipal groundwater systems in the world, drawing from

15:28.430 --> 15:31.130
over 100 wells fed by the Waterloo Moraine aquifer system.

15:31.450 --> 15:35.170
We are, by a wide margin, the largest urban

15:35.670 --> 15:38.810
community in Ontario that relies almost exclusively on groundwater.

15:39.320 --> 15:43.200
And yet, according to provincial records reported by the media, the Ford government has

15:43.700 --> 15:46.760
approved more than 100 water taking permits over the past five years,

15:47.260 --> 15:50.440
allowing commercial and industrial users to draw water from within our region.

15:51.080 --> 15:54.720
Hundreds of additional pre-existing permits remain active. Many of these

15:55.220 --> 15:57.640
permits authorize millions, and in some cases billions,

15:58.440 --> 16:02.040
of litres per year for terms of up to 10 years.

16:02.280 --> 16:05.560
Some of these permits fall into category three, the province's

16:06.060 --> 16:09.330
highest risk classification. Meaning they carry the greatest potential for

16:09.830 --> 16:13.410
unacceptable environmental impact. And here is the most troubling

16:13.910 --> 16:18.850
part. There is no public indication that the government conducted comprehensive environmental

16:19.350 --> 16:22.570
assessments or cumulative water supply capacity

16:23.070 --> 16:26.410
studies before issuing these permits. That responsibility rests with the

16:26.910 --> 16:30.610
provincial ministry of the environment, conservation and parks, a ministry that has made it

16:31.110 --> 16:33.450
easier, not harder, for companies to extract our water.

16:35.490 --> 16:38.770
If anyone doubts that we need stronger protections right now, consider this.

16:39.010 --> 16:42.290
Even as we sit in this chamber discussing a water crisis that has halted

16:42.790 --> 16:46.010
development across the region, a new application for a permit to take water is

16:46.510 --> 16:50.210
under review. And where is this proposed water taking?

16:50.710 --> 16:54.210
In Wilmot Township, the very area where the region is actively exploring

16:54.710 --> 16:58.410
new sites to add drinking water capacity. Citizens for Safe Groundwater have

16:58.910 --> 17:02.210
raised the alarm, noting that the region is pulling from one end of the aquifer

17:02.710 --> 17:06.070
system while a private company seeks to pull from the other. This is precisely

17:06.570 --> 17:10.030
why Councillor Gowing's motion matters. Without the data, without transparency,

17:10.350 --> 17:14.070
without an immediate provincial commitment to pause new water taking

17:14.570 --> 17:18.470
permits in this region, water remains precarious. It is important that

17:18.970 --> 17:22.550
we understand how we got here. The crisis did not emerge

17:23.050 --> 17:26.670
overnight. The Walkerton tragedy of 2000, which killed seven

17:27.170 --> 17:30.390
people and sickened over 2,300, taught Ontario a brutal lesson about

17:30.890 --> 17:34.630
what happens when provincial oversight is gutted and water protections

17:35.130 --> 17:35.870
are treated as expendable.

17:39.390 --> 17:42.510
I want to close with this. Water is not a commodity, it is not a

17:43.010 --> 17:46.390
line item, it is not a resource to be extracted until it is gone

17:46.890 --> 17:50.430
and then puzzled over. Water is life. It is the foundation of public health,

17:50.510 --> 17:54.150
of economic prosperity, of community well-being, and of

17:54.650 --> 17:57.990
the natural ecosystems that sustain us all. Councillor Gowing's motion

17:58.490 --> 18:01.750
is a necessary and overdue step. It will not solve the crisis on its own,

18:02.250 --> 18:05.830
but it will ensure that this council and this community finally has the information it

18:06.330 --> 18:09.970
needs to advocate effectively for prevention It sends a clear message

18:10.470 --> 18:14.330
to Queen's Park that our region demands transparency, accountability, and a moratorium

18:14.830 --> 18:17.770
on commercial water taking permits until the full picture is understood.

18:17.930 --> 18:20.970
I urge you to vote in favor of this motion. Our water, our community,

18:21.050 --> 18:24.410
and our future depend on it. Thank you. Thank you,

18:24.810 --> 18:28.810
Matthew. I see no questions, so thank you for coming today. The next

18:29.310 --> 18:33.090
delegation is Ryan Mounsey, CEO Urban Insights, to talk

18:33.590 --> 18:36.890
about the Mannheim service water capacity. Welcome,

18:37.390 --> 18:39.770
Ryan. You have five minutes.

18:41.210 --> 18:45.290
Thank you, Chair Redman, councillors, industry colleagues.

18:45.930 --> 18:49.730
I'm here today after reviewing the report that was

18:50.230 --> 18:53.610
released the other day. This is about a risk-based

18:54.110 --> 18:57.690
assessment model. We're four months in, now entering five

18:58.190 --> 19:01.930
months in, a full development freeze for new building permits of all sizes.

19:02.870 --> 19:06.550
And the timing of this cannot be emphasized

19:07.050 --> 19:10.390
enough. We need clear risk-based assessment to move

19:10.890 --> 19:14.150
forward, to bring back certainty, not only to the housing market,

19:14.550 --> 19:17.990
but to the property owners, the investors, the construction industry,

19:18.310 --> 19:20.790
to say when are we going to start to build a single new house.

19:21.510 --> 19:24.990
My clients range anywhere from one lot to

19:25.490 --> 19:28.570
1,400 units for a total of 2,500 units in the region of Waterloo.

19:29.280 --> 19:32.680
All those projects are frozen. And so

19:33.180 --> 19:36.800
in understanding, I only work for purpose-built apartment rental developers who

19:37.300 --> 19:40.600
are ready to build. And people that have small infill,

19:41.100 --> 19:44.200
gentle density housing with the modest, the smallest impact on

19:44.700 --> 19:48.000
the housing water impact supply are frozen.

19:49.280 --> 19:52.400
And I'm not going to read my notes. I'm just going to talk just from

19:52.480 --> 19:56.660
what I know. A single house will

19:57.160 --> 20:01.140
have a.008 impact in the

20:01.640 --> 20:04.660
water supply if it was granted to be connected to the water system.

20:05.380 --> 20:08.980
A fourplex will have a.006

20:09.300 --> 20:11.780
impact on the water supply if approved.

20:12.820 --> 20:16.220
Where I'm going with this is that I believe you

20:16.720 --> 20:21.440
are in a position to do phased in resiliency buffers and

20:21.940 --> 20:25.040
to allow the other 50% to accommodate development now.

20:25.920 --> 20:29.520
Small projects should be able to proceed with the limited Tier

20:30.020 --> 20:33.520
1 impact. And then as time progresses and

20:34.020 --> 20:37.320
more water comes on available, then you scale up

20:37.820 --> 20:41.840
the projects. I wrote a report that I've released externally

20:42.160 --> 20:45.640
to talk about the Tier 1, 2, and 3s only as

20:46.140 --> 20:49.460
a concept. Allow development of some form

20:49.960 --> 20:53.420
to go now, I can verify that I'm getting calls from lending institutions

20:53.920 --> 20:57.220
to say what's happening in Waterloo region. I'm receiving calls about that now. And so

20:58.340 --> 21:01.620
what I want to do is have the confidence to say the region

21:02.120 --> 21:05.540
has a plan which you do, the region has a system and a framework to

21:06.040 --> 21:09.380
add more water which you do, but we need guidelines

21:09.880 --> 21:12.980
to say when can you actually pull permits. And I said this in the

21:13.480 --> 21:17.400
last meeting, the region has the hammer. With the local area municipalities. You're not

21:17.900 --> 21:22.040
issuing building permits, oh sorry, you're not issuing water service

21:22.440 --> 21:26.240
permits. So as far as I'm concerned, you can let all

21:26.740 --> 21:30.560
the development approvals go ahead and then based off your criteria start to issue the

21:31.060 --> 21:33.400
building permits based off the criteria.

21:34.520 --> 21:37.960
So that's my summation today is that I want to

21:38.460 --> 21:42.150
stress to you the importance of providing action now Next

21:42.650 --> 21:45.830
month meeting will be six months into the process, half a year without a single

21:46.330 --> 21:49.750
building permit being issued. Surely we can do better. We should

21:50.250 --> 21:53.790
have the rules that allow tier one projects to go ahead now as of

21:54.290 --> 21:57.830
right, scale in concurrently the larger projects

21:58.330 --> 22:02.190
with more than 10 units to 80 units now as the full water capacity

22:02.690 --> 22:06.190
comes online, and allow and phase in the big development projects that can

22:06.690 --> 22:10.990
start construction without a water permit. And that way, you're addressing everyone's

22:11.490 --> 22:14.110
needs for the clients that I serve. I do not sit on Build Urban.

22:14.270 --> 22:17.790
I do not sit on the Waterloo Region Home Builders Association. I'm a

22:18.290 --> 22:20.830
small boutique planning firm of three people in Uptown Waterloo.

22:21.550 --> 22:24.710
And this is my livelihood. 50% of my projects are in Kitchener-Waterloo-Cambridge.

22:25.210 --> 22:28.590
And so I just want to express to you the importance

22:29.230 --> 22:32.670
of coming up with a system now. I see my construction

22:33.170 --> 22:36.630
here that has student housing projects on hold by Conestoga College.

22:37.130 --> 22:41.290
I see innate here that are really ready to build with some projects We're

22:41.790 --> 22:44.930
surrounded by people that have a vested interest. I wanted to be less than

22:45.430 --> 22:49.090
five minutes. I didn't follow my script. And the point is I'm just echoing the

22:49.590 --> 22:52.890
importance of having a system now. That's it. Happy to take any

22:53.390 --> 22:56.290
questions, but there may not be any. So thank you. So, Ryan, don't go anywhere.

22:56.790 --> 23:00.090
There are some questions. Councillor McCabe. Thank you through you, Chair,

23:00.590 --> 23:03.210
and thanks, Ryan, for joining us this morning.

23:04.650 --> 23:08.370
It's not lost on us. I want to reassure you about the critical

23:08.870 --> 23:12.700
importance of this. And the impacts on the development community?

23:12.860 --> 23:18.700
Absolutely. So we want to make sure you're aware of that. We know that this

23:19.200 --> 23:22.300
is really challenging for people with firms of all sizes.

23:23.820 --> 23:28.020
But I just wanted to be clear that, like you've

23:28.520 --> 23:31.660
used the term frozen and development freeze and things of that nature.

23:32.380 --> 23:35.740
But I do want to be clear, at least on behalf of the City

23:36.240 --> 23:39.690
of Waterloo, we're continuing with our planning processes. When we

23:40.190 --> 23:43.090
have to put in a holding provision, we will do that. But there is still

23:43.250 --> 23:47.490
development happening. And I just think, you know, it's really important clarity

23:47.990 --> 23:51.010
of language is really important with anything, but, you know,

23:51.250 --> 23:55.570
particularly in a situation like this. And when I've met with

23:56.050 --> 23:59.250
numerous developers and building consultants

23:59.750 --> 24:03.930
and that, I just, we emphasize, we've answered specific questions

24:04.430 --> 24:08.310
they have. Yes, there is the water constraint issue, but but there

24:08.810 --> 24:12.310
is still the ability to move ahead with, you know how complex

24:12.810 --> 24:16.670
planning is. There's the ability to continue to move ahead with

24:17.950 --> 24:21.270
the bulk of the planning processes. So I just want to

24:21.770 --> 24:24.310
make sure that you're, there's not really, there's not a question there. I just want

24:24.810 --> 24:27.790
to make sure you're aware that that's how us,

24:28.290 --> 24:31.670
and I believe my counterparts in other municipalities like Kitchener and

24:32.170 --> 24:35.110
Woolwich, I don't want to speak for them, but our our proceeding.

24:35.610 --> 24:38.870
So that could have started with a did you know, but did you

24:39.370 --> 24:42.950
know? Did you want to respond because it really wasn't a question? I would

24:43.450 --> 24:46.710
like to, Chair Redman, because this is the misinformation that

24:47.210 --> 24:50.670
is hard for the average person, including lending institutions to figure out as I

24:51.170 --> 24:54.470
receive calls. And I can only talk on behalf of the projects that I

24:54.970 --> 24:57.910
manage, which is 2500 units in Kitchener, Waterloo and Cambridge.

24:58.550 --> 25:01.610
So direct firsthand knowledge. There is a development freeze.

25:02.110 --> 25:06.170
I completely disagree with that position. Development approvals are being processed

25:06.670 --> 25:10.370
at different stages. Official plans and zone changes can go with the holding symbol to

25:10.870 --> 25:14.410
the satisfaction of the regional municipality when water becomes available. So it's

25:14.910 --> 25:18.530
a partial approval, not a full approval. Site plan can get a registered

25:19.030 --> 25:22.250
site plan agreement and get it executed like Kedus right here.

25:22.490 --> 25:25.490
He has a site plan agreement in the City of Waterloo. He can't get a

25:25.990 --> 25:29.090
building permit to move ahead. So while the development approvals can work up to

25:29.590 --> 25:34.040
a certain degree, the development approval process, which includes building permit issuance, is frozen.

25:34.360 --> 25:38.200
And I disagree 100% to say that we're issuing development permits.

25:38.520 --> 25:42.240
Show me where there's a new development permit being activated right

25:42.740 --> 25:46.600
now in the last four months. And there is nothing within my purview that

25:47.100 --> 25:50.440
says that you were proceeding with development approvals that are beyond a net neutral water.

25:51.320 --> 25:54.040
So I don't want to get into an argument about this. I'm happy to talk

25:54.540 --> 25:57.770
to you offline and And I know we've met before,

25:58.270 --> 26:01.850
I forget your name though. And if you wanna meet with our planning staff,

26:02.350 --> 26:06.250
happy to do that, because I don't think

26:06.750 --> 26:10.290
this is not gonna be a helpful environment for debating the nuances of

26:10.790 --> 26:14.370
this. So Councillor McCabe, thank you for that. If you wanna take it offline

26:14.870 --> 26:18.130
and don't go anywhere, Ryan, we have other questions, but I do think it

26:18.630 --> 26:22.250
underscores that we need to keep having conversations. So clarity

26:22.750 --> 26:26.330
will come and focus will come with the more conversations. Councillor James.

26:26.490 --> 26:29.820
Thank you. Thank you through you, Chair Ryan, thank you for coming

26:30.320 --> 26:33.260
in today. Ketus, it's good to see you. Thanks for being here.

26:34.380 --> 26:39.260
Ryan, I just want to, I guess, emphasize that something

26:39.760 --> 26:44.020
you said, and that was around kind of those smaller, you're a boutique

26:44.520 --> 26:49.100
agency. There is smaller developers and builders

26:49.180 --> 26:53.020
that are impacted. You briefly touched on

26:53.520 --> 26:59.010
the calls you're receiving and some of the financial questions and would

26:59.510 --> 27:03.130
you be able to speak to kind of the financial, like that connection

27:03.630 --> 27:07.410
to this? I haven't heard, we've heard a little bit, but what

27:07.910 --> 27:11.930
is the financial industry saying? What are you hearing in the conversations

27:12.430 --> 27:15.530
that you're having? Thank you, Councillor, and through you, Chair.

27:16.490 --> 27:19.890
I've been in business for two years, and what's completely changed

27:20.390 --> 27:23.770
my mindset and how development works is dealing with the financial sides and

27:24.210 --> 27:27.930
the lending institutional side of land development. I spent

27:28.430 --> 27:31.370
20 years in the public sector in Kitchener-Waterloo and the region. And this is an

27:31.870 --> 27:35.490
industry that we're just not closely tied to. And so what my experience has

27:35.990 --> 27:39.810
been is that now people who are investing in projects are coming in

27:40.130 --> 27:43.450
and they are saying, where and why should I

27:43.950 --> 27:47.570
continue to invest in this development proposal? These are all proposals coming in

27:48.070 --> 27:50.530
with applications that I run to get through the development approval process.

27:51.300 --> 27:54.580
Running concurrently is that you need to fund these projects through different

27:55.080 --> 27:58.180
methods. And so now the industries are calling,

27:58.500 --> 28:01.820
institutions are calling, and just saying, we don't know if we want

28:02.320 --> 28:06.180
to lend money to development projects that have undefined timelines

28:06.819 --> 28:10.460
with a permit process because that costs money. And that money and

28:10.960 --> 28:14.260
resource can be put in other markets that can move and make a return on

28:14.760 --> 28:18.100
the investment. So what I can share with you without sharing the institutions

28:18.180 --> 28:21.970
names, These are on the smaller side scale of projects

28:22.450 --> 28:26.450
that need funding to do fourplexes,

28:26.770 --> 28:30.490
to do sixplexes, to do 10plexes. All the gentle density projects that

28:30.990 --> 28:34.450
you want are the ones that are being right now at greatest risk,

28:34.690 --> 28:38.210
which have the lowest impact. My biggest development

28:38.690 --> 28:42.210
project that I have right now, 1,400 units in Kitchener,

28:42.710 --> 28:46.700
is on hold. So that

28:47.200 --> 28:50.220
means no activity on a project that can phase in 1,400 units over time.

28:50.720 --> 28:53.300
So that's just a snapshot. Happy to talk to you offline about more of the

28:53.800 --> 28:57.180
specifics, but it gives you a snapshot of people are now reaching out to me

28:57.680 --> 29:01.100
on that. Okay. Thank you very much, Ryan. Councillor Vrbanovic.

29:02.700 --> 29:06.380
Thank you very much, Ryan. And thank you to you and all the delegations

29:06.880 --> 29:10.540
that have come in today. And there's no question that,

29:10.940 --> 29:15.070
you know, the situation that we're in is providing some

29:15.570 --> 29:18.830
challenging times for everyone. And, you know,

29:19.330 --> 29:22.970
I know for myself and I believe everyone sitting around the regional council

29:23.470 --> 29:26.790
of Horseshoe, there's a commitment to

29:27.290 --> 29:31.070
address this both with some of the motions and reports that

29:31.570 --> 29:34.630
are here today and those that have come before us and those that will follow.

29:34.870 --> 29:38.720
I guess just building on, on, on to your point. And I

29:38.880 --> 29:41.960
do hear your concern around moving forward with projects.

29:42.460 --> 29:46.400
I mean, there are some nuances in terms

29:46.900 --> 29:50.800
of, clearly what you're looking for is a return to

29:51.120 --> 29:55.160
the norm where building permits get processed as part of the process and

29:55.660 --> 29:59.000
move forward. And that's where we all want to get to. I do know

29:59.500 --> 30:03.520
that area municipalities continue

30:03.680 --> 30:07.820
to issue building permits for things

30:08.320 --> 30:12.300
that don't increase water capacity, you know, finishing a basement, a home addition,

30:12.800 --> 30:17.100
things like that. So some of that stuff is happening. It's a larger project set

30:17.600 --> 30:22.700
that obviously are concerned. I guess my question to you is,

30:23.580 --> 30:27.540
you know, going back to a

30:28.040 --> 30:31.340
motion that Regional Council passed previously and staff at the

30:31.840 --> 30:35.430
region and the area municipalities are working on, and that's getting to an environment

30:35.750 --> 30:39.750
where capacity can

30:40.250 --> 30:43.670
allow projects to continue to move forward. How important is

30:44.170 --> 30:47.670
that? Taking that risk management based approach

30:48.790 --> 30:52.550
to the industry in terms of moving things

30:53.050 --> 30:58.790
forward and not seeing many

30:59.290 --> 31:01.670
things come to a halt as you're speaking to right now.

31:03.010 --> 31:05.410
Ryan? Thank you, Chair. Thank you, Mayor.

31:06.530 --> 31:10.930
I'd frame it this way. That original recommendation

31:11.430 --> 31:14.930
for the 50/50 sharing between capacity

31:15.430 --> 31:19.330
allocation for development and resiliency buffer is a very fair

31:19.830 --> 31:23.050
model. It's fair. What's unfair is

31:23.550 --> 31:26.290
about the way this has all unfolded to everyone. The amount of working hours that

31:26.790 --> 31:28.850
you have to invest in this, the amount of time it's taken away from other

31:29.350 --> 31:32.430
projects. But this is one of the biggest economic issues

31:32.930 --> 31:37.070
in one of the regions history. I believe that. And so, Mayor Vrbanovic,

31:37.150 --> 31:40.550
I think that that is a path forward. I think with a tearing

31:41.050 --> 31:44.510
in and a phasing of a resiliency buffer, you can activate development now.

31:45.389 --> 31:49.270
And this sends the message to industry that you are able to

31:49.770 --> 31:53.150
move ahead with some forms of projects. And so leave it

31:53.650 --> 31:56.270
to it. And I'm going to put Rod on the spot for a second.

31:57.130 --> 32:01.170
Is I provide updates weekly, monthly. I reach out

32:01.670 --> 32:05.250
to all the area municipality planning heads and I try to stay in

32:05.750 --> 32:08.250
touch in terms the best of my ability and be the squeaky wheel.

32:09.130 --> 32:12.930
And what I hope to be encouraged by mayor to answer your question is

32:13.430 --> 32:16.290
that this is the most important issue and that's why I'm here today to get

32:16.790 --> 32:20.950
some boundaries and criteria in place. Council for Vrvanovic.

32:21.450 --> 32:25.450
And I certainly don't disagree with you that this is an absolutely critical

32:25.950 --> 32:27.690
issue. Part of the reason why,

32:29.130 --> 32:32.890
for the first time in my memory, other than maybe

32:33.930 --> 32:37.850
some special meetings during COVID that we actually

32:37.930 --> 32:41.650
have set aside on a monthly basis, a whole

32:42.150 --> 32:45.450
set of meetings to clearly focus on this issue.

32:46.170 --> 32:50.010
Just building on that, because one of the things that

32:50.330 --> 32:53.130
we've heard is in the fullness of time,

32:54.050 --> 32:57.530
We're talking about thousands of jobs that potentially are

32:58.030 --> 33:01.490
impacting our community. But it doesn't happen overnight because as we know, there's a lot

33:01.990 --> 33:05.450
of construction projects that are currently being

33:05.950 --> 33:10.050
finished in our region, some that have

33:10.550 --> 33:13.690
had the green light from before and potentially are

33:14.190 --> 33:16.530
starting. But the issue becomes as we go along,

33:17.730 --> 33:21.730
there's different people involved in different parts of the process. So you

33:22.230 --> 33:25.070
sort of start with you know, those that do the grading. And then you get

33:25.570 --> 33:28.590
to the folks that, you know, do the excavating. And then you get to the

33:29.090 --> 33:33.830
folks that, you know, pour the concrete, like the foundational

33:34.330 --> 33:37.710
concrete. And, you know, eventually you get to the people that do

33:38.210 --> 33:41.670
the drywall and the painting and the floors and so on.

33:42.170 --> 33:45.670
And so, you know, while it may not be felt right away, there are people

33:45.750 --> 33:48.840
early in the process that will

33:49.340 --> 33:53.000
start getting impacted sooner. And I think

33:53.500 --> 33:56.160
part of the problem, this is what I've heard from the industry and I'd like

33:56.660 --> 34:01.680
you to confirm or clarify this, is that as

34:02.180 --> 34:06.000
some of those folks potentially start losing work, they will start going

34:06.500 --> 34:09.960
elsewhere and the challenge will be getting those skilled trades

34:10.460 --> 34:13.040
back into the community. I'm wondering if you can speak to that.

34:14.169 --> 34:17.889
Ryan? I can share with you firsthand knowledge that I received to

34:18.389 --> 34:21.409
answer that question, Mayor. There's consulting firms that their

34:21.909 --> 34:25.209
workload is dropped anywhere from 50 to 80% on the consulting side.

34:25.449 --> 34:29.329
So that's immediate. I can confirm to you that in

34:29.829 --> 34:33.009
terms of some of the larger construction companies, as they finish off their jobs right

34:33.509 --> 34:36.809
now, there is no work in the fall and they have over 100 to 200

34:37.309 --> 34:40.329
people on staff. And if there's no work here, what do you do?

34:41.349 --> 34:44.229
There will be an out migration of people to follow work.

34:44.729 --> 34:48.029
There's other markets that are growing and I can confirm to you that this will

34:48.529 --> 34:51.669
have a legacy impact if we don't get construction back to some degree.

34:52.309 --> 34:55.509
The housing side of things, you're talking about personal finances.

34:55.749 --> 34:59.469
I was at a finance event last night and some people can't

34:59.969 --> 35:03.269
get their duplex severed. They put their entire life savings to do this.

35:03.669 --> 35:07.350
And so it's more than than when we

35:07.850 --> 35:11.390
first think the impacts are being felt now and they will intensify and amplify

35:11.890 --> 35:15.310
over time. Okay, thank you very much. Councillor Shantz.

35:16.350 --> 35:19.510
Thank you. Thank you for coming in today and

35:20.010 --> 35:25.030
for all the delegations. I guess I'll

35:25.530 --> 35:27.070
just talk and see if my question comes out.

35:28.910 --> 35:32.150
Maybe a little like you, I'll go office. I won't, I don't have

35:32.650 --> 35:36.150
a script. So we've got a

35:36.650 --> 35:40.070
number of projects in the township that one of them they've been

35:40.570 --> 35:44.110
working on it for 10 years. So that's sort

35:44.610 --> 35:47.870
of in the queue. And I guess

35:48.370 --> 35:51.630
what I'm wondering and what I struggle with because we can use

35:52.130 --> 35:55.910
holding provisions, we could accept other applications and

35:56.410 --> 36:00.430
go through the process, put a hold on it. But at what point

36:01.150 --> 36:04.630
does a developer need to know that you're going

36:05.130 --> 36:06.590
to have water by a certain date?

36:08.750 --> 36:11.310
Do you see where I'm going with that question? Yeah.

36:11.710 --> 36:15.510
So that's a bit of a hypothetical. They tell every politician never to answer.

36:16.010 --> 36:18.670
But Ryan, if you want to be intrepid and try to answer that, go ahead.

36:18.910 --> 36:22.110
I will do my best. And that's

36:22.610 --> 36:26.370
a very clear question. I think what

36:26.870 --> 36:30.570
caught everyone by surprise was, and I'm sorry to

36:31.070 --> 36:35.010
use this word, but it is a freeze. The freeze of approvals

36:35.970 --> 36:39.090
has caught everyone by surprise. And so up until

36:39.590 --> 36:42.450
that point, all the project financing has already been approved.

36:42.850 --> 36:45.890
Up until that point, all the development approval plans are on the way.

36:46.610 --> 36:49.890
And then you're at the tail end of the process, which is the most

36:50.390 --> 36:53.870
impactful, and this is why the urgency is happening right now, the people that

36:54.370 --> 36:58.150
are going through the process. And so you need to know from day one what's

36:58.650 --> 37:02.030
available. Anyone right now may not choose to build

37:02.530 --> 37:05.910
in Waterloo region until there's a clear path in place. So you

37:06.410 --> 37:10.150
just lost that next wave of investment cycles, but the people

37:10.650 --> 37:14.230
who are doing it now are scrambling. They're trying to get new financing. And there's

37:14.730 --> 37:17.630
a condo collapse that happened as well. So all the projects that were on condominium

37:18.130 --> 37:21.550
side of things for many people, there weren't the sales to meet the market

37:22.050 --> 37:25.510
demand and the condo market collapsed. Then you see a rise in rental

37:26.010 --> 37:29.190
and you do see this in your metrics in the CMHC reports.

37:29.830 --> 37:32.830
And so this is good, this is healthy for the market and then it freezes

37:33.330 --> 37:36.430
again. And so in a long-winded way, not as direct

37:36.930 --> 37:40.070
to answer the question with a yes or no, is that you need to know

37:40.570 --> 37:42.870
immediately if you have access to services.

37:44.310 --> 37:47.590
And that is the requirement of applicable law for building permit. Do you have

37:48.090 --> 37:50.510
service? So you can take it all the way up to building permit. And then

37:51.010 --> 37:53.790
at that stage, they'll decide, is it applicable law and do you have access to

37:54.290 --> 37:56.750
services? So you can phase it in,

37:57.150 --> 38:00.470
there, you can phase it in to a certain degree. But this is why

38:00.970 --> 38:04.870
I'm saying you need to have a milestone so

38:05.370 --> 38:08.870
that people can time their investments, time their properties, and say in

38:09.370 --> 38:11.630
six months time, if more water is coming on, they're going to allow a next

38:12.130 --> 38:15.470
block of allocation, then they can finish off their project.

38:16.200 --> 38:18.840
But right now, there's some people in some really compromised positions.

38:20.040 --> 38:23.000
Councillor Shantz, do you have a follow-up? Thank you. Yeah,

38:24.040 --> 38:27.240
I appreciate that. I know I've seen that too.

38:28.280 --> 38:32.039
So really, what you're saying, I think what you're saying

38:32.200 --> 38:35.720
is that we really very quickly need

38:36.220 --> 38:39.760
to have an answer to how much capacity we are going to be able

38:40.260 --> 38:44.930
to release. In the short term to service

38:45.490 --> 38:49.090
projects that are in the queue. Is that

38:49.250 --> 38:52.610
a fair assessment of what you're telling us?

38:52.690 --> 38:55.490
I wish I could have encapsulated that way. That's perfect.

38:55.890 --> 38:59.250
Okay. Thank you very much, Ryan. Those are

38:59.750 --> 39:03.370
all the questions. Sorry, my eScribe's not working for me to log in. Can I

39:03.870 --> 39:06.210
ask a question? Ryan, we have one more question. Councillor Gowing. Thank you.

39:08.590 --> 39:11.870
So coming from 20 plus years in the financial industry,

39:12.190 --> 39:15.670
looking at the developer side, but also looking

39:16.170 --> 39:19.870
at the customer side, who's buying your units.

39:21.070 --> 39:24.510
Have you heard anything from that? Because I know that

39:24.670 --> 39:28.750
stretching out building permits

39:29.250 --> 39:32.070
and things like that, and the closing dates on those units, like I think you

39:32.570 --> 39:35.760
said you had some that are paused at the moment, And if you have people

39:36.260 --> 39:38.320
that have purchased those units,

39:39.360 --> 39:42.760
approvals only last so long when it comes to the construction side of

39:43.260 --> 39:46.720
things. So have you heard from any of your clients whether that is affecting them

39:46.800 --> 39:50.360
at the same time? Ryan,

39:50.860 --> 39:54.360
go ahead. Thank you, Chair. I wish I had more to

39:54.860 --> 39:58.640
answer on that. All my clients are purpose-built rental developers and builders or

39:59.140 --> 40:02.760
homeowners. That market is different than the condominium market.

40:03.080 --> 40:06.240
So I don't know about the closing dates, but what I do know to

40:06.740 --> 40:09.880
your point is that an active building permit stays live for six months,

40:10.120 --> 40:13.680
site plan approval stays live for two years, and there is timelines.

40:14.180 --> 40:18.000
And I'm sure the municipalities would allow for some flexibility given the current context of

40:18.500 --> 40:21.720
what's happening. But I can't answer that question on the customer side.

40:22.440 --> 40:26.200
It's the it's the ownership side and development side that I feel directly.

40:26.470 --> 40:29.750
And it's very difficult right now. Okay,

40:30.310 --> 40:32.710
I'd love to chat with you two offline. Okay, thank you.

40:34.390 --> 40:37.270
So Ryan, I'm now gonna say that's the end of the questions.

40:37.350 --> 40:41.030
So thank you very much. You can return to your seat. Thank you very

40:41.530 --> 40:44.230
much. Thank you for your time. Look forward to the next steps. And sorry if

40:44.730 --> 40:47.750
I came across as combative, but it's coming from a good place. Thank you.

40:49.670 --> 40:52.550
So, Councillor Leggett, I just wanna confirm you can hear us.

40:53.730 --> 40:57.370
Okay, thank you. Colleagues, the next agenda

40:57.870 --> 41:01.490
item is a report on drinking water summary report. I'll now

41:01.990 --> 41:05.770
call on Ken Brothers, Interim Commissioner of Water and Wastewater to speak to

41:06.270 --> 41:09.250
the report. Thank you, Chair Redman.

41:10.530 --> 41:14.330
Regional Council. Today we're seeking council endorsement

41:14.830 --> 41:17.410
of the region's 2025 drinking water summary report.

41:18.780 --> 41:22.260
This report is an annual regulatory obligation under the Safe Drinking

41:22.760 --> 41:26.460
Water Act that requires the operating authority, water utility,

41:26.960 --> 41:30.380
to notify the system owner, which is you, Council, through an annual

41:30.880 --> 41:34.740
drinking water summary report. The summary report details the

41:35.240 --> 41:38.060
compliance summary of our 19 drinking water systems.

41:38.780 --> 41:41.340
Summarizes any non-compliance identified,

41:41.900 --> 41:45.940
outlines water quality and flow rates, Reports on the

41:46.440 --> 41:50.020
annual top management review and any deficiencies and

41:50.520 --> 41:53.860
improvements made. Reports on infrastructure maintenance

41:54.360 --> 41:57.340
plan and highlights with any of the necessary changes.

41:58.300 --> 42:01.620
This report needs to be endorsed by council and posted

42:02.120 --> 42:05.020
on the region's website no later than March 31st of this year.

42:05.980 --> 42:09.580
So at this point I'll pass this over to Director Shaw who will highlight

42:09.900 --> 42:14.250
the details of the report. Thank you, Commissioner Brothers.

42:14.490 --> 42:18.010
The 2025 Drinking Water Summary Report, which is attached

42:18.510 --> 42:22.770
to the report in Appendix A, provides a detailed summary of 17 of

42:23.270 --> 42:27.210
the 19 inspections that were carried out by the MECP in 2025.

42:27.690 --> 42:32.090
The final two inspections were carried out in February of 2026,

42:32.250 --> 42:36.170
and the final reports received after the 2025 summary report

42:36.670 --> 42:40.890
was finalised. This is typical each year with the MECP inspection cycle

42:40.970 --> 42:43.690
running April 1st to March 31st each year.

42:44.650 --> 42:48.970
As a total though, 15 of 19 systems inspected received 100%

42:49.470 --> 42:53.210
compliance score. Four of the 19 systems inspected had a total of

42:53.710 --> 42:57.490
nine non-compliances identified during the inspections. I want

42:57.990 --> 43:01.610
to point out that in light of the water capacity constraint identified in the Mannheim

43:02.110 --> 43:05.330
service area, it is important to note that the municipal drinking water

43:05.830 --> 43:09.250
supplied by the region of Waterloo continues to meet the

43:09.750 --> 43:12.930
necessary requirements under the Safe Drinking Water Act, as can be

43:13.430 --> 43:16.690
seen in the 2025 summary report. This report also

43:17.190 --> 43:21.210
reports back on the requirements for staff to hold an annual top management review

43:21.710 --> 43:25.858
of the drinking water quality management system. That occurred on November 25th,

43:26.002 --> 43:30.240
2025. The management review is intended to provide evidence of

43:30.740 --> 43:34.160
continued endorsement and commitment to the quality management system from

43:34.660 --> 43:37.880
top management. Noncompliance issues are discussed along

43:38.380 --> 43:42.200
with corresponding corrective and preventative actions. The drinking water quality

43:42.700 --> 43:46.480
management system also requires that the operational plan document

43:46.720 --> 43:50.240
a summary and monitor the effectiveness of infrastructure maintenance,

43:50.320 --> 43:54.240
rehabilitation and renewal programs and provide updates to the owner.

43:54.400 --> 43:57.360
That is laid out in detail in the report in Appendix A.

43:58.280 --> 44:01.760
If there's any questions, I've also got Ryan Snyder here as well in

44:02.260 --> 44:05.840
the audience, the manager of water operations. Thank you. Thank you

44:06.340 --> 44:09.000
very much. Councillor Huinink, you have a question? Yes, I do.

44:09.800 --> 44:12.760
Thank you, Chair. Thank you, Mr. Brothers and Ms. Shaw.

44:13.319 --> 44:16.880
In regard to the nine non-compliance issues and

44:17.380 --> 44:20.720
non corrective actions taken, which seemed quite minimal

44:21.220 --> 44:24.920
to me in a good way, I'm just wondering, do we have data

44:25.420 --> 44:30.630
as to whether or not that's comparable to other

44:31.130 --> 44:35.510
municipalities? Are we having more non-compliance issues, less non-compliance

44:36.010 --> 44:39.870
issues? So through the chair, my understanding

44:40.370 --> 44:43.230
is we have less in 2025 than we did in the previous year.

44:43.710 --> 44:47.950
We also have a higher average compliance

44:48.450 --> 44:51.630
score than the average across Ontario. It's higher.

44:51.870 --> 44:55.390
We're just waiting on those final numbers to be tabulated.

44:56.440 --> 44:59.880
But yes, nine is considered good, I would

45:00.380 --> 45:06.920
say. Some of them are administrative, like logbook entries or continuous

45:07.420 --> 45:10.960
monitoring, a little blip in continuous monitoring. So it's those types of

45:11.460 --> 45:16.520
non-compliances. Thanks very much. Councillor Huinink, would you like to move the recommendations?

45:16.840 --> 45:20.200
Sure, I can do that. And a second or seconded by Councillor Wolf.

45:21.730 --> 45:25.290
This is good news, and I. I see that the water team is behind

45:25.790 --> 45:29.290
you, as always. So a good work team. So I'll ask you now to

45:29.790 --> 45:33.610
use your eScribe to vote on the motion before

45:34.110 --> 45:37.449
us, which deals with the 2025 drinking water summary report.

46:14.390 --> 46:18.110
Colleagues, that is carried unanimously, so thank you and that is a good news report.

46:18.610 --> 46:21.830
And again, kudos to our team for staying on top of things.

46:22.070 --> 46:25.550
The next report is a water capacity constraint interim risk

46:26.050 --> 46:29.390
management approach update. And again, I will look to Ken Brothers,

46:29.890 --> 46:32.550
interim commissioner of water and wastewater to introduce the report.

46:33.830 --> 46:36.910
Thank you, Chair Redman. Well,

46:37.410 --> 46:40.150
this is the report, I guess, is a very important stage moving forward.

46:40.650 --> 46:44.090
I think we've all been waiting for this opportunity to advance the thinking

46:44.590 --> 46:48.410
on this. So the purpose of this report is to provide Council with an update

46:48.890 --> 46:52.450
further to the motion passed on January 28th of this year on

46:52.950 --> 46:56.529
the development of an interim risk management framework that once

46:57.029 --> 47:00.410
completed will identify the necessary operational thresholds and

47:00.910 --> 47:04.850
risk-based targets that must be met to allow stage development to

47:05.350 --> 47:08.780
proceed. I'll pass this over to Director Shaw to highlight

47:09.280 --> 47:09.940
some details of the report.

47:14.420 --> 47:17.380
Amy, are you on first or is it Nicole? No, it's me. I'm going to

47:17.880 --> 47:21.140
introduce Nicole. So, yes, thank you through the chair for

47:21.640 --> 47:25.540
having us here today. Today we are here to give council an update on

47:25.620 --> 47:28.740
the amount of work that's been going on in the background since January.

47:29.540 --> 47:32.820
I'm going to call up Nicole Cepeda, who you've all met before.

47:33.320 --> 47:36.500
She's a senior engineer in the Water and Wastewater Infrastructure Management Group.

47:36.920 --> 47:40.720
Who's going to walk us through the presentation on the Interim Risk Management Framework

47:41.220 --> 47:44.600
update. Nicole? Thank you,

47:45.100 --> 47:48.360
Amy. And good morning, Council. Thank you for having Ryan and I here to provide

47:48.860 --> 47:52.160
an update on the Interim Risk Management Framework. Next slide,

47:56.760 --> 48:00.800
As we are all aware, there has been a water capacity constraint identified for the

48:01.300 --> 48:04.530
Mannheim service area. Which supplies drinking water to Kitchener,

48:04.610 --> 48:08.290
Waterloo, and parts of Cambridge, Woolwich, and Wilmot.

48:09.010 --> 48:12.970
Staff are working diligently to address this constraint. Further to the

48:13.470 --> 48:16.650
request from Council, one of the key components of this work is to look at

48:17.150 --> 48:20.450
an interim risk management framework to support decision making with

48:20.950 --> 48:23.650
respect to water servicing for growth. Next slide, please.

48:27.090 --> 48:30.970
The purpose of this presentation is to share an update based on the information that

48:31.470 --> 48:34.760
is currently available. I would like to highlight that work is

48:35.260 --> 48:38.760
ongoing and information is expected to be refined as further progress is

48:39.260 --> 48:42.760
made. A preliminary review was completed of

48:43.260 --> 48:47.360
forecasted development in anticipated new water supply. The review

48:47.860 --> 48:51.360
considered the timing and expected flow rates associated with both development

48:51.520 --> 48:54.800
and the new supply. This review

48:54.880 --> 48:58.830
allows for a preliminary comparison to determine when it is expected that

48:59.330 --> 49:02.390
sustainable operations and operational resiliency could be restored.

49:03.750 --> 49:07.510
A focus was placed on the time period from 2026 to 2028,

49:07.590 --> 49:10.470
as there is a higher level of confidence for this timeframe.

49:11.670 --> 49:14.869
Further, we know based on current demands and available capacity,

49:15.030 --> 49:18.790
this also represents a time period when the local groundwater aquifers

49:19.290 --> 49:22.550
are more likely to be under additional stress based on extended durations for

49:23.050 --> 49:27.080
peaking the supply sources. In parallel with this preliminary

49:27.580 --> 49:31.400
review, an interim risk management framework study has also been initiated, which I will

49:31.900 --> 49:34.880
speak more to later in the presentation. Next slide,

49:34.960 --> 49:37.040
please, and I'll pass the presentation over to Ryan.

49:44.240 --> 49:47.640
So it's been identified that there is a need to understand the timing of future

49:48.140 --> 49:51.060
developments to better understand how much water is needed and when.

49:51.620 --> 49:55.460
While understanding the timing of development is an important consideration in understanding

49:55.960 --> 49:59.140
growth, it is important to note that it is not the only factor that

49:59.640 --> 50:03.060
influences growth. Should we rely solely on the population from new

50:03.560 --> 50:07.300
construction, we miss other factors which are significant drivers of population

50:07.800 --> 50:09.860
change, especially within the Mannheim service area.

50:10.660 --> 50:13.940
The timing of new construction is relatively easy to track once

50:14.440 --> 50:18.180
a building permit has been issued. We've had conversations with our area and municipal planners

50:18.680 --> 50:22.690
and the development stakeholder groups and there's alignment on the average timing of construction

50:23.190 --> 50:27.290
between when a permit is issued and when construction completes. Building completions

50:27.790 --> 50:31.250
represent the visible growth. We see construction happening, we see the people

50:31.750 --> 50:35.170
moving into their homes. However, it's just the one factor that influences growth.

50:36.530 --> 50:39.810
To get an accurate representation of the anticipated growth over the

50:40.310 --> 50:43.570
next few years, we have to look at the additional factors that impact growth,

50:43.650 --> 50:47.150
and there's a few key drivers here. The growth of

50:47.650 --> 50:51.190
multi-generational families. We are seeing a rise of an increased number of people

50:51.690 --> 50:54.950
living under one roof together. This means that without any new construction,

50:55.110 --> 50:59.190
we are seeing additional population within our region. Housing turnover.

50:59.350 --> 51:03.030
We're seeing older residents downsizing, making room for new families.

51:03.350 --> 51:06.870
Old homes are shifting occupancy from two empty nesters to a family

51:07.370 --> 51:10.390
of four. Student population fluctuations.

51:10.630 --> 51:14.470
Our three post-secondary institutions are located within the Mannheim service area.

51:14.970 --> 51:18.370
And over the past five years, we've seen a significant swing in those student

51:18.870 --> 51:20.330
populations. We can go to the next slide.

51:25.130 --> 51:28.250
To better account for the full picture of growth that is anticipated to occur in

51:28.750 --> 51:32.650
the Mannheim service area, we have prepared a preliminary forecast for the Mannheim service area

51:33.150 --> 51:36.730
from 2025 to 2028. We have reliable information

51:36.810 --> 51:40.250
to inform the growth in 2025. We have information

51:40.330 --> 51:44.170
from the post-secondary institutions on their fall 2025 enrollments.

51:44.610 --> 51:48.290
And we know how many homes completed construction. For 2026

51:48.790 --> 51:52.090
to 2028, we relied on historical population growth as a

51:52.590 --> 51:56.930
proxy for anticipated growth in the future. For now, this assessment

51:57.430 --> 52:01.410
excluded student populations. As we know, given the recent federal policy announcements,

52:01.490 --> 52:04.530
we will not be seeing a significant growth of student populations,

52:04.770 --> 52:07.890
especially compared to the growth seen over the last five years.

52:09.010 --> 52:12.580
We see growth fluctuate up and down. This method incorporates the

52:13.080 --> 52:16.740
factors mentioned on the previous slide and provides an average growth rate we

52:17.240 --> 52:21.740
may see over the next few years. With the known market conditions across

52:22.240 --> 52:25.820
Ontario, the forecast as presented could be seen as optimistic.

52:26.540 --> 52:29.900
We have also initiated discussions with both the planning focused water

52:30.400 --> 52:34.180
servicing capacity working group and the water

52:34.680 --> 52:37.940
supply developer stakeholder group to better understand the growth over the next few

52:38.440 --> 52:41.920
years. Both groups identified concerns with assessing growth based

52:42.420 --> 52:45.840
on estimating the timing of future developments. We are exploring the

52:46.340 --> 52:49.600
various temporal lenses we can apply to better understand the anticipated

52:50.100 --> 52:53.480
growth over the next few years. We anticipate developing a more

52:53.980 --> 52:57.640
refined forecast to accompany the finalised risk-based assessment.

52:58.200 --> 53:01.760
I also want to highlight that within the appendix of the report you

53:02.260 --> 53:05.900
will find a summary of the updated 2025 planning application data within the

53:06.400 --> 53:09.660
Mannheim service area. We are continuing to work with our area municipal

53:10.160 --> 53:13.140
partners to refine the data. As information is refined and updated,

53:13.640 --> 53:17.060
we'll be sure to update council. We can go to the next slide and I'll

53:17.560 --> 53:18.340
pass it back to Nicole.

53:23.300 --> 53:25.140
Thank you, Ryan.

53:26.340 --> 53:29.940
New water supply solutions are needed to sustainably operate the water system.

53:30.400 --> 53:32.960
Restore operational resiliency, and support growth.

53:33.760 --> 53:37.600
A preliminary list of projects that can provide sustainable water supply capacity

53:38.100 --> 53:41.440
has been developed and work is ongoing through the technical focused water servicing

53:41.940 --> 53:44.800
capacity working group to confirm short and medium term opportunities.

53:45.840 --> 53:49.320
This table provides an overview of the forecasted new water supply

53:49.820 --> 53:52.160
anticipated between 2026 and 2028.

53:53.040 --> 53:57.080
All of these projects combined represent new water supply capacity of

53:57.580 --> 54:01.200
up to approximately 350 litres per second. Next slide

54:01.700 --> 54:05.960
please. The forecasted

54:06.460 --> 54:09.280
development timing was translated into anticipated water demands.

54:10.000 --> 54:13.880
The first row of this table provides an overview of these water demands expressed

54:14.380 --> 54:17.760
as average day demands. The second row provides an

54:18.260 --> 54:21.760
overview of the total sustainable water supply capacity anticipated based on

54:22.260 --> 54:26.210
the preliminary list of projects for new water supply. The total

54:26.710 --> 54:30.210
sustainable water supply capacity is a representation of what the system could

54:30.710 --> 54:32.890
produce on an ongoing basis if everything was online.

54:34.410 --> 54:38.050
As discussed in previous presentations to Council, it is impossible to

54:38.550 --> 54:42.610
keep all facilities running non-stop. Planned and unplanned shutdowns are

54:43.110 --> 54:47.610
a reality of operating a water system. The available sustainable

54:48.110 --> 54:51.490
water supply capacity represents what the system could produce when the minimum

54:51.990 --> 54:55.910
operational resiliency is considered. This is equal to 80% of

54:56.410 --> 54:58.030
the total sustainable water supply capacity.

54:59.310 --> 55:03.230
To operate sustainably and restore operational resiliency, the available

55:03.310 --> 55:06.750
sustainable water supply capacity should be greater than the average day

55:07.250 --> 55:10.750
demands. Based on the anticipated new water supply

55:11.250 --> 55:14.750
capacity, sustainable operations and the operational resiliency

55:15.250 --> 55:17.310
are not expected to be restored until 2027.

55:18.430 --> 55:19.310
Next slide please.

55:22.370 --> 55:25.970
This slide presents the information from the previous slide in a more visual way.

55:27.010 --> 55:30.250
All of the data shown is the same, but we can see more clearly how

55:30.750 --> 55:35.010
the forecasted supply and demand compare to each other. Green represents

55:35.510 --> 55:39.530
the average day demands, light blue represents the total sustainable water supply

55:40.030 --> 55:43.610
capacity, and dark blue represents the available sustainable water supply

55:44.110 --> 55:47.700
capacity. When looking at the information this way, the objective remains

55:48.200 --> 55:52.220
the same. The available sustainable water supply should be

55:52.720 --> 55:56.700
greater than the average day demands. When looking at this graph, this happens

55:57.200 --> 56:00.819
when the navy blue bar is greater than the green bar. As indicated

56:01.319 --> 56:03.900
in the previous slide, this is forecasted to occur in 2027.

56:05.180 --> 56:06.060
Next slide, please.

56:09.020 --> 56:12.100
An interim risk management framework study has been initiated to

56:12.600 --> 56:16.260
review how stage development could potentially proceed under certain risk space conditions

56:16.760 --> 56:20.860
in the Mannheim service area. The focus is to look at how much

56:21.360 --> 56:24.900
water could potentially be made available to support growth while we work to fully

56:25.400 --> 56:27.980
restoring sustainable operations and operational resiliency.

56:29.020 --> 56:32.300
This path forward must look at opportunities to manage risks

56:32.800 --> 56:34.540
while protecting the existing drinking water system.

56:35.660 --> 56:38.380
There are many considerations for a risk management framework.

56:39.100 --> 56:42.220
In preliminary discussions, these have been categorized as three

56:42.720 --> 56:45.690
key themes. Demand risk management, operational risk management,

56:45.930 --> 56:49.250
and supply risk management. Common questions

56:49.750 --> 56:53.690
that staff have heard from council, delegates, and the public include things like,

56:53.850 --> 56:56.650
do we need 20% operational resiliency right away,

56:56.810 --> 57:00.530
or could this be staged? Can we continue to peak our

57:01.030 --> 57:04.330
supply wells to support growth? And what role

57:04.830 --> 57:08.570
does water efficiency and conservation play? This study is

57:09.070 --> 57:12.410
collecting data and information to answer questions like these using an

57:12.910 --> 57:16.130
evidence-based approach to assess risk and potential mitigation measures.

57:17.170 --> 57:20.810
It is important to note that the Interim Risk Management Framework Study will not be

57:21.310 --> 57:23.010
assessing how water would be allocated to development.

57:24.370 --> 57:27.889
Preliminary discussions are underway with the planning-focused water servicing

57:28.389 --> 57:31.690
capacity working group to discuss how potential water capacity for growth could

57:32.190 --> 57:34.290
be allocated and the roles in allocations.

57:35.330 --> 57:38.610
As these discussions progress, we anticipate further discussions

57:39.040 --> 57:42.080
with other working groups. Next slide, please.

57:44.960 --> 57:48.480
The project team for the Interim Risk Management Framework Study is meeting weekly

57:48.980 --> 57:53.240
to progress this work. The study will be leveraging outcomes from ongoing initiatives and

57:53.740 --> 57:55.039
working groups to streamline efforts.

57:58.400 --> 58:02.480
Where necessary, supporting studies by technical experts have also been initiated.

58:02.880 --> 58:06.200
For example, a study by technical experts is now in progress to

58:06.700 --> 58:10.210
assess the risks of continuing to operate the drinking water wells at their peak flow

58:10.710 --> 58:14.170
rates to potentially support stage development. The targeted

58:14.670 --> 58:18.090
completion for the Interim Risk Management Framework Study is May 2026,

58:18.250 --> 58:21.610
which is considered to be an aggressive timeline based on the amount of work

58:21.930 --> 58:25.730
that is needed to complete, needs to be completed to support

58:26.230 --> 58:29.770
this critical risk-based decision making. While all efforts will

58:30.270 --> 58:34.250
be made to expedite this work, additional time may be required for some technical components.

58:35.300 --> 58:38.540
If the work is not fully completed by May 2026, Regent staff

58:39.040 --> 58:42.620
will report back with an interim update on progress and an updated timeline for

58:43.120 --> 58:46.340
completion. Staff understand the importance of this work.

58:46.580 --> 58:49.540
We are working diligently to expedite wherever feasible.

58:50.820 --> 58:54.100
Thank you for providing the opportunity to bring this update forward to Council today.

58:58.500 --> 59:02.100
Thank you very much. Don't go anywhere. We've got lots of questions.

59:02.600 --> 59:03.300
Councillor Vrbanovic.

59:06.680 --> 59:10.960
Thank you very much and thank you to staff for the

59:11.460 --> 59:15.160
presentation. I guess a couple questions to start off with

59:15.660 --> 59:18.600
and we have more later. When we go back to the population,

59:19.800 --> 59:23.360
we only go back to 2024 and

59:23.860 --> 59:27.960
I'm wondering if we should actually go back farther so that we actually

59:28.120 --> 59:31.840
see what the trend line has been,

59:33.360 --> 59:37.160
you know, it may actually be helpful and

59:37.660 --> 59:41.000
quite frankly, information that, you know, would potentially become part

59:41.500 --> 59:45.080
of the kind of report that Councillor Craig is

59:45.580 --> 59:49.600
asking for later in today's meeting. But seeing what those

59:50.100 --> 59:53.520
population numbers have been so that we understand how they relate to sort

59:54.020 --> 59:57.640
of water in that time period, and I'm thinking, you know, maybe a 10-year

59:58.140 --> 01:00:01.830
trend wouldn't make sense here. And in

01:00:02.330 --> 01:00:05.990
particular, I also think that we should, I understand that you didn't

01:00:06.490 --> 01:00:09.870
include the student numbers, but I actually think it would be good to see

01:00:10.370 --> 01:00:14.070
them because there were things that started changing with student

01:00:14.570 --> 01:00:16.934
numbers around 2016,

01:00:17.152 --> 01:00:21.070
17, 18, somewhere in that time period.

01:00:21.310 --> 01:00:24.110
I think we can see them go up and now we know that they've gone

01:00:24.610 --> 01:00:28.530
down significantly. And I raise that because surely as we

01:00:29.030 --> 01:00:32.730
look at this, the fact that one of our institutions

01:00:33.230 --> 01:00:35.890
is seeing 25,000 less students,

01:00:36.370 --> 01:00:39.970
that's impacting usage both

01:00:40.470 --> 01:00:43.850
at the facilities and no doubt residentially in

01:00:44.350 --> 01:00:47.650
our community. So I think that needs to be

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:52.980
better understood so that we we

01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:57.540
factor it in the long run. So is that data that could be provided in

01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:01.460
a subsequent report? Ryan, do you want to respond to that? Yes,

01:01:01.620 --> 01:01:05.700
through the chair. We absolutely can provide that. I believe the January

01:01:06.260 --> 01:01:09.540
report included information going back to 2020,

01:01:09.860 --> 01:01:14.060
but we can go back 10 years. We can separate out students and regular population

01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:17.460
and provide that growth out to 2028.

01:01:18.430 --> 01:01:22.230
Okay, thank you, thank you for that. And then the second question is

01:01:22.730 --> 01:01:26.030
just on this, you know, as we look

01:01:26.530 --> 01:01:30.310
at the risk-based approach, you know,

01:01:30.810 --> 01:01:33.070
one of the things that I think may merit,

01:01:34.590 --> 01:01:37.750
you know, considerate, and I believe it is being discussed

01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:42.310
by the working group, is this notion of looking

01:01:42.810 --> 01:01:46.650
at certain, servicing at occupancy versus servicing at

01:01:47.150 --> 01:01:49.850
building permit in order to allow projects to proceed.

01:01:49.930 --> 01:01:53.850
Because we know there will be projects, like many of these projects will take two

01:01:54.350 --> 01:01:56.650
to three years to be constructed.

01:01:57.050 --> 01:02:01.010
And so, you know, if the servicing is

01:02:01.510 --> 01:02:04.010
required at building permit stage, that's a problem.

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:10.450
But in terms of holding things up, but if it's

01:02:10.950 --> 01:02:14.800
really required at occupancy permit, that would change things quite dramatically.

01:02:15.200 --> 01:02:19.040
And so I guess I just want to encourage staff

01:02:19.520 --> 01:02:24.160
to really be looking at that going forward because

01:02:24.660 --> 01:02:28.040
it's very different than building a single family home, for example, which could be up

01:02:28.540 --> 01:02:28.720
in a few months.

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:34.800
And so I'm just wondering if there's any initial thinking around that.

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:39.660
So it looks like Nicole is willing to answer that

01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:43.220
and I'll put you back in the queue, Councillor Vrbanovic, after this. Yep,

01:02:43.720 --> 01:02:47.220
thank you. Through the chair, thank you

01:02:47.720 --> 01:02:52.060
for that feedback. I think it is a really important consideration and there's two

01:02:52.560 --> 01:02:56.020
streams of work that are happening so that we can expedite this work as

01:02:56.520 --> 01:03:00.140
quickly as possible. So there is the Interim Risk Management Framework Study, which is

01:03:00.640 --> 01:03:04.540
really trying to answer the question of how much capacity could be

01:03:05.040 --> 01:03:08.400
made available from a water capacity perspective to support stage

01:03:08.900 --> 01:03:12.200
development. And then there's this question of how does that

01:03:12.700 --> 01:03:16.040
capacity get allocated to the development and through the work

01:03:16.540 --> 01:03:20.200
that's ongoing with the planning focused water servicing capacity working group.

01:03:20.760 --> 01:03:24.480
I think part of what you've highlighted might be covered

01:03:24.980 --> 01:03:28.400
under that, but regardless, those two initiatives are working closely together to

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:31.720
share information and to make sure that we're covering off those considerations.

01:03:32.770 --> 01:03:36.130
Thank you very much. Councillor Huinink. Thank you, Chair Redman.

01:03:36.290 --> 01:03:39.730
For the benefit of everyone here, I want to reiterate that the regional water team

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:43.090
and the working groups are working really, really, really hard.

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:47.410
I understand that the timelines might not be ideal for

01:03:47.910 --> 01:03:51.250
the development industry, and there are repercussions of that, but I don't

01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:55.010
know how we encourage them to work harder or

01:03:55.510 --> 01:03:57.570
faster when they're going as fast as they can.

01:03:59.560 --> 01:04:02.920
Until now, here's my question which I'm going to

01:04:03.420 --> 01:04:05.560
put into the room and have no idea who's going to answer it.

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:10.880
Until now it kind of seems as though water protection

01:04:11.380 --> 01:04:15.040
advocates are against the development industry and vice versa. And the

01:04:15.540 --> 01:04:18.920
truth of the matter is, for the vitality of water in the region,

