02:08:03.860 --> 02:08:06.720
and why that 20% is actually at a negative level right now?

02:08:09.200 --> 02:08:13.000
So through the chair, it comes down to that chart that Ryan Snyder

02:08:13.500 --> 02:08:17.160
had showed us at the last council meeting. I believe at the

02:08:17.660 --> 02:08:20.480
time of that presentation, we had 208 litres per second offload.

02:08:21.190 --> 02:08:24.630
So there was the Greenbrook, the Parkway, there was in two other wells

02:08:24.790 --> 02:08:27.190
that added up to 208 litres per second.

02:08:28.710 --> 02:08:32.070
The demand is also higher than the sustainable capacity,

02:08:32.710 --> 02:08:36.429
which adds to that. So with Parkway and Greenbrook offline,

02:08:36.929 --> 02:08:39.750
that's 140 litres per second that's now gone.

02:08:40.710 --> 02:08:44.790
And so that does put us in a deficit of not having

02:08:44.870 --> 02:08:48.480
enough operational resilience. So when everything is online, I think it was

02:08:48.980 --> 02:08:52.840
6.9%. Again, I'm just going based on my brain power

02:08:53.340 --> 02:08:57.240
here. But right now we're at a negative. So once those come back

02:08:57.740 --> 02:09:00.720
online, it's great because it gives us a little bit of breathing room and allows

02:09:01.220 --> 02:09:04.000
us to not push those supply wells as hard.

02:09:04.720 --> 02:09:08.480
But again, I know it's confusing, but just

02:09:08.560 --> 02:09:12.120
the ones that are offline right now will not be

02:09:12.620 --> 02:09:16.250
net new capacity. But any new projects like the modular sides stream,

02:09:16.490 --> 02:09:19.810
the Wilmot 30 litres per second, all of those are considered

02:09:20.310 --> 02:09:23.636
new capacity. Just one quick thing on that.

02:09:24.136 --> 02:09:27.370
So I appreciate that. But when we say that,

02:09:27.870 --> 02:09:30.730
okay, great, this is coming on in March, this is coming on in the fall,

02:09:31.130 --> 02:09:34.970
it makes everybody excited that there's new capacity. If we're showing

02:09:35.450 --> 02:09:38.810
that capacity currently, when realistically it's not there.

02:09:39.310 --> 02:09:43.010
So it just sounds like it's new capacity. So I think We need

02:09:43.510 --> 02:09:46.530
to be conscious of that when we get it out to the public saying these

02:09:47.030 --> 02:09:50.650
are new things coming on or back online, but in fact there's nothing new coming.

02:09:51.290 --> 02:09:54.370
And through the chair, we've tried in one of our presentations to have them

02:09:54.870 --> 02:09:58.209
in separate charts where it said no net new capacity and those were listed.

02:09:58.709 --> 02:10:01.090
And then we have the chart below where it was new capacity and we tried

02:10:01.590 --> 02:10:04.330
to break them out. And in the white paper it is broken out that way.

02:10:04.830 --> 02:10:07.770
So we try and make it a little more clear in big letters that those

02:10:08.270 --> 02:10:11.370
two those two projects are not in net new capacity. So appreciate that.

02:10:12.010 --> 02:10:15.610
Thanks, Amy. Councillor Huinick. Thank you, Chair.

02:10:17.530 --> 02:10:20.970
My question has a similar premise to Councillor Gowing,

02:10:21.470 --> 02:10:26.010
